      {
        "name": "PATIENT_INFORMATION",
        "heading": "PATIENT INFORMATION",
        "text": "Patient Name: Rohan Iyer\nPatient Identifier: NEW-PATIENT-001 (External Synthetic ID)\nDate of Birth: 17-Nov-1997 (Age: 27)\nGender: Male\nBlood Group: At\nContact Number: 9876543210\nEmail: rohan. iyer@fictional.demo\nAddress: Flat 12B, Orchid Residency, Indiranagar, Bengaluru, Karnataka\nREASON FOR FOLLOW-UP\nScheduled neurology follow-up visit, approximately eight months after initial\nconsultation. Patient was first seen on 18 July 2024 for evaluation of\nrecurrent headaches with migrainous features. An MRI Brain was subsequently\nperformed and returned to clinic for review and ongoing management.\nPREVIOUS INVESTIGATIONS\nMRI Brain (with and without contrast) — dated 25 July 2024 (Accession No.:\nMRI-2024-0725-1148, Sanjeevan Neurosciences Clinic Imaging Centre):\nRESULT: No acute intracranial abnormality identified. No infarction,\nhaemorrhage, or space-occupying lesion. Normal MRI of the brain.\nIncidental finding: Mild right maxillary sinus mucosal thickening\n(clinically insignificant).\nRoutine Blood Work (performed July 2024):\nCBC: Within normal limits.\nESR: 8 mm/hr (normal).\nCRP: 0.4 mg/L (normal).\nTSH: 2.1 mIU/L (normal, reference 0.44.0).\nINTERVAL HISTORY\nSince the initial consultation in July 2024, the patient reports a meaningful\nimprovement in headache frequency and severity. Over the past eight months:\n- Headache frequency has reduced from 2-3 episodes per week to approximately\n2-3 episodes per month.\n- Headache intensity has decreased from moderate-severe (6—-7/10 on VAS) to\nmild-moderate (3-4/10 on VAS).\n- Duration of individual episodes has also shortened (now typically 2—4 hours,\npreviously 4-12 hours).\n- Photophobia is now rare and limited to the more intense episodes only.\nThe patient attributes the improvement to:\n- Implementation of sleep hygiene recommendations (now sleeping approximately\n7 hours per night, up from 5-6 hours).\n- Reduced screen time during non-work hours.\n- Maintaining a regular exercise routine (walking 30 minutes, 4-5 days/week)\n- Reduced caffeine intake (from 3 cups to 1 cup of coffee per day).\n- Maintaining a headache diary as advised.\nThe patient was started on a low-dose preventive medication (Propranolol) by\nDr. Subramaniam during a brief telephone review in November 2024, after\nheadache frequency had plateaued at approximately once per week.\nNo episodes of sudden thunderclap headache. No seizures. No visual aura. No\nfocal neurological symptoms. No syncope. No new symptoms reported.",
        "start": 459,
        "end": 2916
      },
      {
        "name": "MEDICATIONS",
        "heading": "CURRENT MEDICATIONS",
        "text": "Medication | Dose | Route | Frequency | Status | Notes\nween newman enn neha nnn nahn nna nanan n abana nanan nna nanan aaa\nPropranolol | 20 mg [| Oral | OD (AM) | Active | Preventive; started Nov 2024\nParacetamol | 500 mg | Oral | PRN | Active | Acute headache relief; max 2g/day\nNo other prescription medications.\nNo known drug allergies.",
        "start": 2935,
        "end": 3276
      },
      {
        "name": "EXAMINATION",
        "heading": "PHYSICAL EXAMINATION",
        "text": "General: Well-appearing, relaxed, in no acute distress.\nVitals:\nBlood Pressure: 116/72 mmHg (right arm, sitting)\nHeart Rate: 66 bpm, regular (note: on Propranolol)\nRespiratory Rate: 14/min\nTemperature: 36.7°C\nWeight: 73 kg (previous: 74 kg)\nBMI: 23.8 kg/m?\nNEUROLOGICAL EXAMINATION\nMental Status: GCS 15/15. Alert, oriented to time, place, and person.\nCranial Nerves: CN I-XII intact. PERRL 3 mm bilaterally. No papilloedema.\nVisual fields full. Facial symmetry preserved.\nMotor: Power 5/5 all four extremities. Tone normal.\nSensory: Intact to light touch, pinprick, vibration, proprioception.\nReflexes: 2+ bilaterally and symmetric. Plantars flexor bilaterally.\nCoordination: Finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin normal. No dysmetria.\nGait: Normal gait and tandem walk.\nNeurological examination remains unremarkable with no focal deficit.",
        "start": 3296,
        "end": 4132
      },
      {
        "name": "ASSESSMENT",
        "heading": "ASSESSMENT",
        "text": "27-year-old male with a diagnosis of migraine without aura, initially\npresenting in July 2024 with recurrent episodic headaches. MRI Brain (July\n2024) was normal, excluding secondary intracranial pathology.\nThere has been a clinically significant improvement in headache frequency (from\n2-3/week to 2-3/month) and severity over the past eight months. The improvement\nis attributable to a combination of lifestyle modifications and low-dose\nPropranolol prophylaxis.\nCurrent headache burden is manageable and the patient is satisfied with his\nsymptom control.\nNo red-flag neurological features. Neurological examination remains normal.",
        "start": 4142,
        "end": 4777
      },
      {
        "name": "PLAN",
        "heading": "PLAN",
        "text": "1. MEDICATION:\n\n- Continue Propranolol 20 mg OD (morning) for migraine prophylaxis.\n\n- Continue Paracetamol 500 mg PRN for breakthrough headaches.\n\n- Monitor resting heart rate; patient counselled to report bradycardia\nsymptoms (dizziness, fatigue, presyncope).\n\n2. LIFESTYLE:\n\n- Continue current lifestyle modifications (sleep hygiene, exercise,\nreduced caffeine, limited screen time).\n\n- Continue maintaining headache diary.",
        "start": 4781,
        "end": 5209
      },
      {
        "name": "FOLLOW_UP",
        "heading": "3. FOLLOW-UP",
        "text": "- Review in 6 months (approximately September 2025).\n\n- If headaches remain well-controlled at next visit, consider gradual\ntapering of Propranolol.\n\n- If headache frequency increases or new symptoms develop, return sooner.\n\n- No repeat neuroimaging indicated at this time given clinical improvement\nand previously normal MRI.\n\n4. PATIENT COUNSELLING:\n\n- Reassured that brain MRI was normal and the headache pattern is\nconsistent with primary migraine.\n\n- Advised to present to the emergency department if experiencing sudden\nsevere headache (\"worst headache of life\"), seizures, visual loss, or\nany new focal neurological symptoms.\n\nPHYSICIAN SIGNATURE\n\nElectronically signed by:\n\nDr. Meenakshi Subramaniam, DM Neurology\n\nSenior Consultant, Department of Neurology\n\nSanjeevan Neurosciences Clinic, Bengaluru\n\nDate: 12 March 2025\n\nSanjeevan Neurosciences Clinic — \"Compassionate Neurological Care\"\nPage 1 of 1",
        "start": 5223,
        "end": 6134
      }
    ],
